1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,140
Meet Maya.

2
00:00:02,340 --> 00:00:04,388
She trains artificial intelligence in a

3
00:00:04,388 --> 00:00:05,224
lab in Montreal,

4
00:00:05,224 --> 00:00:06,589
and this morning her phone won't stop

5
00:00:06,589 --> 00:00:07,230
buzzing.

6
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:08,697
Headline after headline says the same

7
00:00:08,697 --> 00:00:09,460
thing.

8
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:12,530
Canada's economy is crashing.

9
00:00:12,550 --> 00:00:14,880
Then a message from California.

10
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:16,840
A big AI lab wants her.

11
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:18,218
Bigger paycheck,

12
00:00:18,218 --> 00:00:19,877
bigger computers,

13
00:00:19,877 --> 00:00:20,940
more sunshine.

14
00:00:20,940 --> 00:00:22,526
Pack your bags,

15
00:00:22,526 --> 00:00:23,940
it says.

16
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:25,056
Meanwhile,

17
00:00:25,056 --> 00:00:26,081
far to the south,

18
00:00:26,081 --> 00:00:27,107
in the desert,

19
00:00:27,107 --> 00:00:29,520
somebody else is packing too.

20
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:30,870
This is Whirr.

21
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:32,461
Whirr is a data center,

22
00:00:32,461 --> 00:00:34,340
and Whirr is overheating.

23
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:35,696
Its fans are screaming,

24
00:00:35,696 --> 00:00:37,511
its power bill keeps climbing,

25
00:00:37,511 --> 00:00:39,440
and it needs a new home.

26
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:41,700
Maya is thinking of moving south.

27
00:00:41,700 --> 00:00:43,880
Whirr is thinking of moving north.

28
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:45,350
One of them has read the map

29
00:00:45,350 --> 00:00:45,920
wrong.

30
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:47,240
So which is it?

31
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:49,314
Is Canada losing the AI boom,

32
00:00:49,314 --> 00:00:51,199
or could it be the biggest winner

33
00:00:51,199 --> 00:00:52,218
of all?

34
00:01:07,283 --> 00:01:08,396
To be fair,

35
00:01:08,396 --> 00:01:10,393
the bad news is real.

36
00:01:10,393 --> 00:01:12,709
Back in the spring of twenty twenty-five,

37
00:01:12,709 --> 00:01:15,259
American tariffs hit Canadian cars and

38
00:01:15,259 --> 00:01:16,300
machinery,

39
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:17,128
exports fell,

40
00:01:17,128 --> 00:01:19,803
and the economy actually shrank.

41
00:01:19,803 --> 00:01:22,133
Maya's uncle sells car parts.

42
00:01:22,133 --> 00:01:24,313
He stopped smiling at the news.

43
00:01:24,313 --> 00:01:26,253
The headlines did the math.

44
00:01:26,253 --> 00:01:27,479
Crash.

45
00:01:27,717 --> 00:01:30,687
But crashes don't usually bounce.

46
00:01:30,837 --> 00:01:33,358
This year the economy grew again,

47
00:01:33,358 --> 00:01:35,670
and the Bank of Canada expects more

48
00:01:35,670 --> 00:01:36,572
growth ahead,

49
00:01:36,572 --> 00:01:38,653
even if new tariffs slow things down

50
00:01:38,653 --> 00:01:39,947
this winter.

51
00:01:40,367 --> 00:01:41,577
That's a forecast,

52
00:01:41,577 --> 00:01:42,937
not a promise.

53
00:01:42,937 --> 00:01:43,501
Still,

54
00:01:43,501 --> 00:01:46,477
it's not the picture on Maya's phone.

55
00:01:46,717 --> 00:01:48,635
And while everyone stared at the

56
00:01:48,635 --> 00:01:49,383
headlines,

57
00:01:49,383 --> 00:01:51,497
something else was happening.

58
00:01:51,497 --> 00:01:54,237
The AI boom went shopping.

59
00:01:54,287 --> 00:01:56,148
And its shopping list looks a lot

60
00:01:56,148 --> 00:01:58,377
like a map of Canada.

61
00:01:58,617 --> 00:02:01,362
Let me draw what an AI factory

62
00:02:01,362 --> 00:02:02,237
needs.

63
00:02:02,237 --> 00:02:03,618
Start with a plug:

64
00:02:03,618 --> 00:02:06,249
huge amounts of electricity,

65
00:02:06,249 --> 00:02:08,147
all day and all night.

66
00:02:08,147 --> 00:02:09,140
Then a fridge:

67
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:11,718
every chip turns power into heat,

68
00:02:11,718 --> 00:02:14,447
and the heat has to go somewhere.

69
00:02:14,497 --> 00:02:15,460
Then a mine:

70
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:15,895
chips,

71
00:02:15,895 --> 00:02:19,017
cables and batteries are made of metal.

72
00:02:19,097 --> 00:02:20,280
And finally,

73
00:02:20,280 --> 00:02:21,053
a brain:

74
00:02:21,053 --> 00:02:22,984
people who know how to build the

75
00:02:22,984 --> 00:02:24,067
models.

76
00:02:24,117 --> 00:02:26,179
Most countries are strong in a couple

77
00:02:26,179 --> 00:02:27,197
of these.

78
00:02:27,197 --> 00:02:30,032
Very few have the whole list.

79
00:02:34,483 --> 00:02:35,854
Whirr heads north first,

80
00:02:35,854 --> 00:02:38,653
following the power lines.

81
00:02:38,653 --> 00:02:40,707
They lead out of the mountains and

82
00:02:40,707 --> 00:02:41,966
down the rivers,

83
00:02:41,966 --> 00:02:44,363
to dams the size of small towns.

84
00:02:44,493 --> 00:02:46,287
Of all its electricity,

85
00:02:46,287 --> 00:02:48,908
Canada makes about six of every ten

86
00:02:48,908 --> 00:02:51,323
kilowatt hours from falling water.

87
00:02:51,433 --> 00:02:54,218
Whirr has never seen anything like it.

88
00:02:54,483 --> 00:02:56,958
That makes Canada the third-biggest

89
00:02:56,958 --> 00:03:00,033
hydropower maker on the planet.

90
00:03:00,103 --> 00:03:02,233
Whirr does a little dance.

91
00:03:02,233 --> 00:03:02,795
Clean,

92
00:03:02,795 --> 00:03:03,653
steady,

93
00:03:03,653 --> 00:03:05,732
and already built.

94
00:03:05,983 --> 00:03:06,975
In Quebec,

95
00:03:06,975 --> 00:03:09,863
the power is famous for being cheap.

96
00:03:09,933 --> 00:03:11,838
A big factory there pays less than

97
00:03:11,838 --> 00:03:13,955
a quarter of what the same factory

98
00:03:13,955 --> 00:03:15,613
would pay in Boston.

99
00:03:15,943 --> 00:03:17,694
We told that story in Could Cheap

100
00:03:17,694 --> 00:03:20,483
Electricity Become the New Oil?

101
00:03:20,813 --> 00:03:22,883
Whirr starts unpacking.

102
00:03:23,150 --> 00:03:25,280
Then comes the twist.

103
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:27,823
Hydro-Québec says its days of surplus

104
00:03:27,823 --> 00:03:29,610
power are over.

105
00:03:29,870 --> 00:03:32,093
Big new users now need the government's

106
00:03:32,093 --> 00:03:32,960
permission,

107
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:34,961
and the price it has proposed for

108
00:03:34,961 --> 00:03:37,184
data centers is more than double what

109
00:03:37,184 --> 00:03:39,340
big industry pays today.

110
00:03:39,490 --> 00:03:40,928
It's still a proposal,

111
00:03:40,928 --> 00:03:42,570
still under review.

112
00:03:42,750 --> 00:03:44,918
Whirr stops unpacking.

113
00:03:45,183 --> 00:03:45,937
Next door,

114
00:03:45,937 --> 00:03:47,732
in British Columbia,

115
00:03:47,732 --> 00:03:50,332
big AI and data center projects no

116
00:03:50,332 --> 00:03:53,533
longer simply join the line for power.

117
00:03:53,703 --> 00:03:55,533
They now have to compete for a

118
00:03:55,533 --> 00:03:56,654
limited slice,

119
00:03:56,654 --> 00:03:58,004
like a talent show,

120
00:03:58,004 --> 00:03:59,834
judged on what they give back to

121
00:03:59,834 --> 00:04:01,120
the province.

122
00:04:01,383 --> 00:04:03,455
So Whirr learns the first lesson of

123
00:04:03,455 --> 00:04:04,503
the north.

124
00:04:04,503 --> 00:04:06,213
Canada has the power.

125
00:04:06,213 --> 00:04:06,969
But the cheap,

126
00:04:06,969 --> 00:04:09,703
easy power is already spoken for.

127
00:04:09,833 --> 00:04:12,165
The next power has to be built,

128
00:04:12,165 --> 00:04:15,124
and dams and power lines take years.

129
00:04:19,583 --> 00:04:20,103
Still,

130
00:04:20,103 --> 00:04:22,566
the north has one gift nobody has

131
00:04:22,566 --> 00:04:23,613
to build.

132
00:04:23,613 --> 00:04:24,901
Whirr steps outside,

133
00:04:24,901 --> 00:04:27,241
and for the first time in ages,

134
00:04:27,241 --> 00:04:28,893
its fans slow down.

135
00:04:28,943 --> 00:04:30,090
In the desert,

136
00:04:30,090 --> 00:04:32,432
a data center spends a fortune on

137
00:04:32,432 --> 00:04:34,073
air conditioning.

138
00:04:34,073 --> 00:04:34,553
Up here,

139
00:04:34,553 --> 00:04:35,711
the cold air does a lot of

140
00:04:35,711 --> 00:04:36,953
that work for free.

141
00:04:36,953 --> 00:04:38,313
Whirr sighs.

142
00:04:38,313 --> 00:04:39,853
Ahhh.

143
00:04:40,117 --> 00:04:42,867
One Quebec operator says it can cool

144
00:04:42,867 --> 00:04:45,117
its servers with outside air most of

145
00:04:45,117 --> 00:04:46,137
the year.

146
00:04:46,137 --> 00:04:47,205
That's its own claim,

147
00:04:47,205 --> 00:04:49,165
and it depends on how a building

148
00:04:49,165 --> 00:04:50,637
is designed.

149
00:04:50,787 --> 00:04:52,637
But the idea is simple.

150
00:04:52,637 --> 00:04:54,257
The cold is a giant,

151
00:04:54,257 --> 00:04:55,955
natural fridge.

152
00:04:56,217 --> 00:04:58,269
And the heat that comes out of

153
00:04:58,269 --> 00:05:01,177
the servers doesn't have to be wasted.

154
00:05:01,387 --> 00:05:02,109
In Kamloops,

155
00:05:02,109 --> 00:05:03,200
British Columbia,

156
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:04,866
the phone company Bell plans an AI

157
00:05:04,866 --> 00:05:07,271
data center at a university that will

158
00:05:07,271 --> 00:05:09,307
pipe its leftover heat into the campus

159
00:05:09,307 --> 00:05:10,037
buildings.

160
00:05:10,037 --> 00:05:12,059
The servers keep the students warm.

161
00:05:12,317 --> 00:05:14,457
The giants have noticed.

162
00:05:14,517 --> 00:05:17,295
Microsoft has announced plans worth almost

163
00:05:17,295 --> 00:05:20,577
fourteen billion US dollars in Canada over

164
00:05:20,577 --> 00:05:21,562
a few years,

165
00:05:21,562 --> 00:05:24,687
mostly for cloud and AI data centers.

166
00:05:24,937 --> 00:05:26,451
That's a company plan,

167
00:05:26,451 --> 00:05:28,587
not money already spent.

168
00:05:28,587 --> 00:05:30,584
But it's a big bet on the

169
00:05:30,584 --> 00:05:31,470
north.

170
00:05:31,717 --> 00:05:33,649
Let me draw why the cold is

171
00:05:33,649 --> 00:05:35,077
worth money.

172
00:05:35,137 --> 00:05:36,517
Here's a server.

173
00:05:36,547 --> 00:05:37,629
Power goes in,

174
00:05:37,629 --> 00:05:39,615
and almost all of it comes out

175
00:05:39,615 --> 00:05:40,657
as heat.

176
00:05:40,847 --> 00:05:42,162
In a hot place,

177
00:05:42,162 --> 00:05:43,289
you pay twice:

178
00:05:43,289 --> 00:05:44,872
once to run the chip,

179
00:05:44,872 --> 00:05:47,887
and again to pump the heat away.

180
00:05:48,067 --> 00:05:49,419
In a cold place,

181
00:05:49,419 --> 00:05:51,902
the outside air carries much of the

182
00:05:51,902 --> 00:05:54,157
heat off almost for free.

183
00:05:54,267 --> 00:05:55,576
And if you're lucky,

184
00:05:55,576 --> 00:05:57,507
a neighbor wants that heat.

185
00:05:57,517 --> 00:05:58,483
Same chip,

186
00:05:58,483 --> 00:06:00,083
smaller bill.

187
00:06:00,317 --> 00:06:02,607
Whirr makes up its mind.

188
00:06:02,607 --> 00:06:03,309
Cold air,

189
00:06:03,309 --> 00:06:04,254
clean power,

190
00:06:04,254 --> 00:06:06,277
and neighbors who want it.

191
00:06:06,277 --> 00:06:08,604
It starts looking for a lot.

192
00:06:13,050 --> 00:06:13,996
Meanwhile,

193
00:06:13,996 --> 00:06:15,913
Maya goes home for the weekend,

194
00:06:15,913 --> 00:06:17,101
to Sudbury,

195
00:06:17,101 --> 00:06:18,047
Ontario,

196
00:06:18,047 --> 00:06:18,994
where her dad,

197
00:06:18,994 --> 00:06:19,454
Luc,

198
00:06:19,454 --> 00:06:21,510
works at a nickel mine.

199
00:06:21,570 --> 00:06:23,610
He has heard about California.

200
00:06:23,790 --> 00:06:26,260
You know what's inside those AI computers?

201
00:06:26,260 --> 00:06:27,420
he asks.

202
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:28,410
Rocks.

203
00:06:28,410 --> 00:06:29,839
Our rocks.

204
00:06:30,083 --> 00:06:32,053
He's not wrong.

205
00:06:32,063 --> 00:06:33,673
Copper for the wires.

206
00:06:33,673 --> 00:06:34,197
Nickel,

207
00:06:34,197 --> 00:06:36,553
lithium and graphite for the batteries.

208
00:06:36,553 --> 00:06:38,494
Uranium for the nuclear plants that could

209
00:06:38,494 --> 00:06:40,453
power the data centers.

210
00:06:40,523 --> 00:06:42,599
Canada's government keeps an official list

211
00:06:42,599 --> 00:06:44,847
of minerals the modern economy can't do

212
00:06:44,847 --> 00:06:45,793
without.

213
00:06:45,853 --> 00:06:47,412
There are thirty-four on it,

214
00:06:47,412 --> 00:06:49,986
and Canada mines many of them.

215
00:06:50,250 --> 00:06:51,345
In uranium,

216
00:06:51,345 --> 00:06:53,580
Canada is the world's second-biggest

217
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:54,850
producer.

218
00:06:54,870 --> 00:06:56,250
Luc grins.

219
00:06:56,250 --> 00:06:56,693
Rocks,

220
00:06:56,693 --> 00:06:58,226
he says again.

221
00:06:58,483 --> 00:07:01,203
But here's the catch with rocks.

222
00:07:01,353 --> 00:07:02,783
They're slow.

223
00:07:03,123 --> 00:07:04,242
Around the world,

224
00:07:04,242 --> 00:07:06,069
a big new mine has taken more

225
00:07:06,069 --> 00:07:07,187
than sixteen years,

226
00:07:07,187 --> 00:07:08,306
on average,

227
00:07:08,306 --> 00:07:10,590
to go from discovery to the first

228
00:07:10,590 --> 00:07:12,103
truckload of ore.

229
00:07:12,483 --> 00:07:13,290
By then,

230
00:07:13,290 --> 00:07:16,950
Maya's AI models would be ancient history.

231
00:07:17,217 --> 00:07:19,712
Ottawa has set up a Major Projects

232
00:07:19,712 --> 00:07:22,127
Office to speed up approvals.

233
00:07:22,627 --> 00:07:25,017
One copper and zinc mine in Saskatchewan

234
00:07:25,017 --> 00:07:27,625
was referred to it and started producing

235
00:07:27,625 --> 00:07:29,124
less than a year later,

236
00:07:29,124 --> 00:07:31,897
although it was already mostly built.

237
00:07:32,087 --> 00:07:33,837
Fast for a mine.

238
00:07:34,227 --> 00:07:37,105
Whether that becomes normal is the real

239
00:07:37,105 --> 00:07:37,966
test.

240
00:07:42,417 --> 00:07:43,567
Back in Montreal,

241
00:07:43,567 --> 00:07:45,186
Maya walks into her lab,

242
00:07:45,186 --> 00:07:47,532
and you can see why California wants

243
00:07:47,532 --> 00:07:48,357
her.

244
00:07:48,357 --> 00:07:50,502
Some of the big ideas behind today's

245
00:07:50,502 --> 00:07:51,767
AI grew up right here,

246
00:07:51,767 --> 00:07:53,890
in Canadian universities,

247
00:07:53,890 --> 00:07:55,606
when most of the world had given

248
00:07:55,606 --> 00:07:56,849
up on them.

249
00:07:57,083 --> 00:07:58,175
In Toronto,

250
00:07:58,175 --> 00:08:00,180
Geoffrey Hinton spent decades on

251
00:08:00,180 --> 00:08:02,162
artificial neural networks,

252
00:08:02,162 --> 00:08:03,722
when much of the field thought they

253
00:08:03,722 --> 00:08:05,213
were a dead end.

254
00:08:05,373 --> 00:08:07,082
In twenty twenty-four,

255
00:08:07,082 --> 00:08:09,312
that work helped earn him the Nobel

256
00:08:09,312 --> 00:08:10,903
Prize in Physics.

257
00:08:11,150 --> 00:08:12,155
In Montreal,

258
00:08:12,155 --> 00:08:14,731
Yoshua Bengio founded the lab that grew

259
00:08:14,731 --> 00:08:15,994
into Mila,

260
00:08:15,994 --> 00:08:18,313
which describes itself as the world's

261
00:08:18,313 --> 00:08:21,405
largest academic research center for deep

262
00:08:21,405 --> 00:08:22,520
learning.

263
00:08:22,560 --> 00:08:25,558
Maya's lab is just down the street.

264
00:08:25,817 --> 00:08:28,294
Canada was also the first country in

265
00:08:28,294 --> 00:08:30,321
the world to launch a national AI

266
00:08:30,321 --> 00:08:31,597
strategy.

267
00:08:31,707 --> 00:08:33,463
And now it's paying to build a

268
00:08:33,463 --> 00:08:36,196
giant AI supercomputer of its own,

269
00:08:36,196 --> 00:08:38,301
owned in Canada.

270
00:08:38,550 --> 00:08:40,890
So where's the problem?

271
00:08:41,140 --> 00:08:43,200
Follow Maya's classmates.

272
00:08:43,200 --> 00:08:45,047
Most science and tech graduates do stay

273
00:08:45,047 --> 00:08:46,053
in Canada,

274
00:08:46,053 --> 00:08:48,090
Statistics Canada finds.

275
00:08:48,230 --> 00:08:50,378
But the stars get the biggest offers,

276
00:08:50,378 --> 00:08:52,764
and many of those come from American

277
00:08:52,764 --> 00:08:55,100
companies with deeper pockets.

278
00:08:55,280 --> 00:08:57,771
Promising Canadian startups often find

279
00:08:57,771 --> 00:08:58,881
their biggest buyers,

280
00:08:58,881 --> 00:09:00,218
and their biggest checks,

281
00:09:00,218 --> 00:09:01,950
south of the border.

282
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:03,880
Maya's phone buzzes.

283
00:09:03,880 --> 00:09:05,450
The recruiter again.

284
00:09:05,450 --> 00:09:06,820
Decided yet?

285
00:09:06,870 --> 00:09:08,208
Not yet.

286
00:09:08,450 --> 00:09:11,560
Let me draw Canada's talent pipeline.

287
00:09:11,560 --> 00:09:12,795
Students go in here,

288
00:09:12,795 --> 00:09:14,720
at the universities.

289
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:16,545
They come out as researchers,

290
00:09:16,545 --> 00:09:18,300
and then as founders.

291
00:09:18,350 --> 00:09:19,790
But look at this pipe.

292
00:09:19,790 --> 00:09:20,952
There's a leak,

293
00:09:20,952 --> 00:09:22,186
and it points south:

294
00:09:22,186 --> 00:09:23,111
higher pay,

295
00:09:23,111 --> 00:09:24,511
bigger computers,

296
00:09:24,511 --> 00:09:26,000
bigger funding.

297
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,440
Canada doesn't need to stop the water.

298
00:09:28,440 --> 00:09:30,192
It needs to give it reasons to

299
00:09:30,192 --> 00:09:30,674
stay:

300
00:09:30,674 --> 00:09:32,623
computers to work on at home,

301
00:09:32,623 --> 00:09:34,813
and companies big enough to grow up

302
00:09:34,813 --> 00:09:35,632
here.

303
00:09:40,083 --> 00:09:42,371
Which brings us to the biggest catch

304
00:09:42,371 --> 00:09:43,543
of all.

305
00:09:43,723 --> 00:09:46,917
Canada's best customer is also its biggest

306
00:09:46,917 --> 00:09:47,763
risk.

307
00:09:47,853 --> 00:09:50,457
Of every dollar of goods Canada sells

308
00:09:50,457 --> 00:09:51,087
abroad,

309
00:09:51,087 --> 00:09:53,474
nearly three of every four dollars go

310
00:09:53,474 --> 00:09:55,593
to the United States.

311
00:09:55,633 --> 00:09:57,939
When Washington adds tariffs,

312
00:09:57,939 --> 00:10:00,290
Canada feels it first.

313
00:10:00,550 --> 00:10:03,140
AI could change that list.

314
00:10:03,140 --> 00:10:06,122
Electricity turned into computing doesn't

315
00:10:06,122 --> 00:10:07,370
ride on trucks.

316
00:10:07,370 --> 00:10:09,520
It travels through cables.

317
00:10:09,620 --> 00:10:11,267
That could be a new kind of

318
00:10:11,267 --> 00:10:11,950
export,

319
00:10:11,950 --> 00:10:14,538
but only if Canada builds the data

320
00:10:14,538 --> 00:10:15,221
centers,

321
00:10:15,221 --> 00:10:17,339
and only if someone at home owns

322
00:10:17,339 --> 00:10:19,586
the companies that use them.

323
00:10:19,850 --> 00:10:22,020
So here's the scorecard.

324
00:10:22,020 --> 00:10:22,856
Power:

325
00:10:22,856 --> 00:10:23,614
plenty,

326
00:10:23,614 --> 00:10:25,679
but the cheap part is taken,

327
00:10:25,679 --> 00:10:28,370
and new dams and lines take years.

328
00:10:28,430 --> 00:10:29,248
Cold:

329
00:10:29,248 --> 00:10:30,220
free.

330
00:10:30,290 --> 00:10:31,049
Metals:

331
00:10:31,049 --> 00:10:31,974
in the ground,

332
00:10:31,974 --> 00:10:33,760
but slow to dig up.

333
00:10:33,770 --> 00:10:34,626
Brains:

334
00:10:34,626 --> 00:10:35,754
world class,

335
00:10:35,754 --> 00:10:37,910
but tempted south.

336
00:10:38,060 --> 00:10:39,088
And next door,

337
00:10:39,088 --> 00:10:41,987
a giant neighbor that can slow everything

338
00:10:41,987 --> 00:10:43,905
down with a tariff.

339
00:10:48,350 --> 00:10:48,822
So,

340
00:10:48,822 --> 00:10:51,555
could Canada be the biggest winner of

341
00:10:51,555 --> 00:10:52,900
the AI boom?

342
00:10:53,030 --> 00:10:54,657
It has something rare:

343
00:10:54,657 --> 00:10:56,216
the whole shopping list,

344
00:10:56,216 --> 00:10:57,720
in one country.

345
00:10:57,790 --> 00:10:58,670
Power,

346
00:10:58,670 --> 00:10:59,246
cold,

347
00:10:59,246 --> 00:11:01,060
metals and brains.

348
00:11:01,080 --> 00:11:02,831
Most countries would trade a lot for

349
00:11:02,831 --> 00:11:03,650
that.

350
00:11:03,750 --> 00:11:05,619
But having the list isn't the same

351
00:11:05,619 --> 00:11:06,920
as winning.

352
00:11:06,980 --> 00:11:09,253
Canada wins only if it builds:

353
00:11:09,253 --> 00:11:10,345
power lines,

354
00:11:10,345 --> 00:11:10,991
mines,

355
00:11:10,991 --> 00:11:12,083
data centers,

356
00:11:12,083 --> 00:11:14,089
and companies that grow up at home

357
00:11:14,089 --> 00:11:16,249
instead of moving away.

358
00:11:16,483 --> 00:11:19,223
And that crash everyone talks about?

359
00:11:19,283 --> 00:11:21,151
It's real for some industries,

360
00:11:21,151 --> 00:11:23,643
like the car parts hit by tariffs.

361
00:11:23,853 --> 00:11:25,357
But the AI boom is betting on

362
00:11:25,357 --> 00:11:27,273
a different Canada.

363
00:11:27,293 --> 00:11:29,152
The one behind the dams,

364
00:11:29,152 --> 00:11:30,472
under the snow,

365
00:11:30,472 --> 00:11:32,419
and inside the labs.

366
00:11:32,683 --> 00:11:33,986
Back in Montreal,

367
00:11:33,986 --> 00:11:37,177
Maya opens the California offer one more

368
00:11:37,177 --> 00:11:38,043
time.

369
00:11:38,073 --> 00:11:40,213
Then she looks out the window.

370
00:11:40,343 --> 00:11:41,428
Across the street,

371
00:11:41,428 --> 00:11:43,177
a new building is going up,

372
00:11:43,177 --> 00:11:45,612
and a friendly data center waves at

373
00:11:45,612 --> 00:11:46,433
her.

374
00:11:46,433 --> 00:11:47,583
It's Whirr.

375
00:11:47,583 --> 00:11:48,428
I moved in,

376
00:11:48,428 --> 00:11:49,213
it says.

377
00:11:49,213 --> 00:11:50,533
Your turn.

378
00:11:50,633 --> 00:11:52,463
Maya types her answer.

379
00:11:52,603 --> 00:11:54,533
Ask me again next year.

380
00:11:54,533 --> 00:11:55,583
For now,

381
00:11:55,583 --> 00:11:56,907
I'm staying.

382
00:11:57,150 --> 00:11:57,757
Next time,

383
00:11:57,757 --> 00:12:00,476
we follow the money behind another hidden

384
00:12:00,476 --> 00:12:02,750
link of the world economy.

385
00:12:02,930 --> 00:12:05,004
This is The Hidden Economics.
